WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:02.460
Speaker 1: All right, why don't we try Nick
from New York next.

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.040
Nick, you're on the air.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.600
Speaker 2: Hi, David.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.600
Hello.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.550
Speaker 1: Oh, I'm doing well, thank you.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:13.730
Speaker 2: I was just wondering I've been
trying to ask you this question.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:19.780
What is the best way to pass the voting rights
legislation without the Supreme Court striking

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:20.870
it down?

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:24.360
Speaker 1: Why would the Supreme Court strike
it down?

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.340
Speaker 2: Like H.R. one, you know, conservatives
like to argue that it's unconstitutional.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:30.340
Right.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:35.100
Speaker 1: But conservatives often argue that
things are unconstitutional and they're just

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.899
wrong.

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:41.230
Yeah, what do they say is unconstitutional
about H.R. one?

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:50.350
Speaker 2: They say that while they force
states to, you know, change their redistricting

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:57.460
rules and, you know, make them have all these
sorts of things having to do with

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:02.460
Speaker 1: my position on this is make them
prove it's unconstitutional, make them actually

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:08.460
do it, because just Republicans saying it's
unconstitutional to me, it's just not good

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.460
enough.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.200
And if they actually want to fight that, make
them do the work.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.890
I don't think it's a reason not to push for
H.R. one.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:21.440
And I hope it passes and put Republicans in
the position of having to actually sue and

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.940
appeal and appeal and appeal.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:24.659
I wouldn't be scared away by that.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:31.040
Speaker 2: Well, what makes the federal government
mandating that states change their election

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.040
laws constitutional?

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.420
Speaker 1: Well, the problem is the framing.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:43.280
So Republicans frame it as H.R. one would
be the federal government telling states how

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.720
to run elections.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:51.420
There's a completely different way to understand
it, which is the federal government, much

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:58.409
like with the Voting Rights Act, which was
completely constitutional, can set basic parameters

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:05.549
and guidelines that must be met by states
for their constitutions to be considered fair

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:09.509
and valid, and that held with the Voting Rights
Act.

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:13.309
And there is no reason to believe in particular
that it wouldn't hold with H.R. one.

00:02:13.309 --> 00:02:16.999
I mean, maybe aspects of H.R. one would be
found unconstitutional.

00:02:16.999 --> 00:02:19.999
But the argument you're repeating is not a
very strong one.

00:02:19.999 --> 00:02:28.739
Speaker 2: Yeah, I'm simply just trying to,
you know, debunk these these is talking, of

00:02:28.739 --> 00:02:29.739
course.

00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:30.739
Speaker 1: Of course.

00:02:30.739 --> 00:02:33.049
Yeah, no, I just make them prove it.

00:02:33.049 --> 00:02:34.219
Make them prove it is the point.

00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:43.459
Speaker 2: OK, and I would like to ask you
another thing, OK, um, what is, um.

00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:49.790
I am wondering why don't you mention how Joe
Manchin and Kirsten Sinema are bought out

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:50.790
by corporations?

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.609
I don't see you talk about that.

00:02:52.609 --> 00:02:53.609
Speaker 1: We do.

00:02:53.609 --> 00:02:59.019
I mean, I know less about cinema, but Joe
Manchin and West Virginia business interests

00:02:59.019 --> 00:03:01.540
are something we have talked about before.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:07.909
I think maybe if you're not hearing me talk
about it as much recently, it's sort of I

00:03:07.909 --> 00:03:11.489
mean, it's not as much a part of the big picture.

00:03:11.489 --> 00:03:15.339
If you have something where there are even
more Democrats that aren't on board or you

00:03:15.339 --> 00:03:16.339
need 60.

00:03:16.339 --> 00:03:20.209
So it's not even about just Democrats, but
at least as far as Joe Manchin goes, we have

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:21.209
talked about that.

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:22.699
Speaker 2: All right.

00:03:22.699 --> 00:03:26.489
Speaker 1: Yeah, all right, my friend, thank
you very much for the call.

00:03:26.489 --> 00:04:35.919
I appreciate it.

